= The primary care diagnosis and management of osteoarthritis
== OARSI World Congress, Vienna
#smallcaps[Date]\ August 2024

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Abbott JH, Dowell T, MacRae J, Wilson R, Darlow B. The primary care diagnosis and management of osteoarthritis. _Osteoarthritis Cartilage_ 2024; 32(S1):S73. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joca.2024.02.786\")[10.1016/j.joca.2024.02.786]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
*Purpose (the aim of the study)*
Osteoarthritis (OA) is very common in adults, and its morbidity burden may
begin during early- and mid-stage disease. Very little is known about
patterns of healthcare encounters and utilization in New Zealand (NZ)
primary care of people with OA.

This project aimed to access and interrogate routinely collected patient
encounter data in individual primary care electronic health records (EHRs)
held by general medical/family practices in the lower North Island of NZ,
identify cases and non-cases of hip or knee OA, and report the associated
diagnosis, treatment, prescription and referral patterns.

*Methods*
In this retrospective observational cohort study, we defined a ten-year
period extending from 1 Jan 2009 to 31 December 2018. From 31 participating
practices, we accessed over 75 Million raw EHR entries including 7M
diagnostic and clinical coding records (Read codes), 17M prescribing
records and 20M encounter records. Matching between practice data sets was
based on the NZ National Health Index (NHI) number. We included patients
who met the following criteria: 1) adults aged 40 years or older, 2)
enrolled at any practice for a 5-year contiguous period; and 3) had at
least one recorded clinical note in the EHR during the study period.

Demographics for date of birth, gender, ethnicity, date of death, and
socioeconomic deprivation were based on the most recent enrolment register
file available within the study period. We identified 79,861 eligible
patients’ EHRs (683,060.25 patient-years of EHR data). We identified OA
cases using Read codes augmented with free-text annotations (indicating hip
or knee OA). We excluded cases with a general OA Read code not indicating
hip or knee OA. Eligible patients with no OA Read code in the EHR were
defined as non-cases of OA.

We report age- and sex-matched comparisons of healthcare utilisation in
primary care over the 10-year period following the recording of an OA
diagnosis in primary care EHRs, and we linked these primary care data with
the Statistics NZ Integrated Data Infrastructure (IDI) to also report
hospital-based healthcare use (specialists appointments, outpatient
physiotherapy, and joint replacement surgery) in the NZ public healthcare
system in the 4 years before and after the recording of a hip or knee OA
diagnosis in primary care EHRs.

*Results*
From an overall cohort of 69,663 adults in primary care, we identified 4599
people with hip or knee OA (57.7% knee), mean age 65.8 (11.3), 56.5%
female, 6.5% Māori (Indigenous people of NZ). After adjusting for NZ
population demographics, the mean (95% CI) rate of hip or knee OA was 0.13
(0.128 to 0.134). Māori had greater odds of OA diagnosis [around 1.3 (1.1
to 1.6) across age bands].

Age- and sex-matched comparisons showed significantly higher healthcare
consumption in the OA group over the study period with increased primary
care use, prescribing, hospital outpatient visits and surgery. People with
a hip or knee OA Read code had 32% more primary care contacts than those
without OA (135 vs 102).

88.9% (88.0 to 90.4) of OA cases were dispensed NSAIDs compared with 72.1%
of non-cases, with paracetamol and weak opiate rates similar. Strong
opiates were dispensed to 21.6% (20.5 to 22.8) of OA cases, double that of
the non-OA cohort. 39.3% of OA cases had a first specialist appointment in
a public hospital; 24.1% having joint replacement surgery (compared with
9.5%; 1.4% in non-cases). Outpatient physiotherapy was accessed by 47.8%
(95% CI 46.4 to 49.3) of people with an OA code compared with 19.1% of
non-cases.

Across all categories of healthcare consumption we saw a similar pattern of
excess use evident and rising steadily in the 4 years prior to the first
recording of an OA Read code in the primary care EHR. Furthermore, we saw
absence of an OA Read code in primary care even after joint replacement
surgery for an OA indication in secondary care in over 50% of surgeries
(25% recorded in primary care only after surgery, 28% not at all).

*Conclusions*
OA diagnosis coding in primary care was associated with excess healthcare
consumption. Patterns of OA care were not entirely consistent with
guideline-recommended practice. We saw strong evidence of under-reporting
of OA (by Read code) in the primary care record; these findings may be
biased downward via under-recording and late recording of OA diagnosis in
primary care EHRs.

